<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isla Mujeres</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isla Mujeres is a small island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> Sea off the north-east coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán Peninsula</a>, opposite Cancún, in the Mexican state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintana-roo">Quintana Roo</a>. The Maya had a shrine there to the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ix-chel">Ixchel</a>, and Spaniards of Francisco Hernández de Córdoba's expedition named it in 1517 after the female figures they found. It is now a tourist resort.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isla-mujeres.jpg" alt="Isla Mujeres" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Šarūnas Burdulis from USA · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birds_eye_view_of_Isla_Mujeres_(4257542920).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
